\f4\fs26 It took Cracker 35 minutes to get down to the ground floor, some 216 floors below Sarah\'92s office and 100 floors below the layer. There had been a major delay when an L.5. suit decided to slum it with the workers and got in the service elevator at level 100. Without a word he entered his code and the lift began ascending again, straight back to floor 216. Cracker could smell a mild scent of something burning, hair or skin perhaps, but it dissipated as soon as the L.5 scurried off the lift. Cracker was able to get a glimpse of him entering Sarah\'92s office before the lift door closed. He figured this guy must be a drama queen. Apart from a suicide, there nothing out of the ordinary on floor 216. \
Upon hitting the pavement, he unconsciously looked up, somehow expecting to see Sarah at her window waving at him, but his view was blocked by the layer of thick cloud and she was not waving at him. She was dealing with the suspicious looking character that just stepped off the service-lift and was presently sleezing around in her office. He was quite tall and seemed to impose himself in her office, rather than merely occupy it and was breathing loudly, too loudly to account for the short run from the elevator. His anxiety, even though he was clearly attempting to keep a lid on it, was nevertheless palpable as he fidgeted and scratched the dark black locks on his distorted angular head. \
\'93Can I help you?\'94 She accused.\
\'93I am looking for Mr. Lee. He has some files for me to sign.\'94 The dark haired man did not sound convincing, not many document were actually signed anymore. Paper cost too much and it was an extremely inefficient method of data storage. \
\'93What kind of files?\'94\
\'93Contracts, torts and legal things, 
\f5\i Res ipsa loquitor.
\f4\i0 \'94\
\'93Mmm.\'94 She said gravely. \'93That does not sound very likely. This is the Cyber Security Division. We sometimes deal with legal matters, but never without company lawyers present and please don\'92t use legal terms you don\'92t understand. You are going to have to try a bit harder than that.\'94 
\f5\i Contracts and torts and legal things? Res Ipsa Loquitor? How dumb does he think I am? 
\f4\i0 She was always profoundly insulted when men made the mistake of assuming she was as dull as them. \'93Mr. Lee has passed away. His files will be re-allocated in due course.\'94 She looked at the open doorway but the dark man did not budge.\
\'93I need to see the files now.\'94 He was clearly in a hurry and allowed a little hostility to creep out in an effort to underline that urgency.\
\'93If, indeed, you do have business with this department you will be contacted in due course.\'94 Sarah remained neutral and professional, but was concerned about the true nature of his business with the recently deceased Mr. Lee. \'93I do not have them and I cannot help you. I think you will find the door is behind you. Good day.\'94\
Wave after wave of white hot rage washed over the dark-haired clown. His infantile ego had been dented and this could not go unanswered. The idea of his agenda being frustrated by some idiotic computer jockey infuriated him and an old mantra began repeating inside his head. 
\f6\i\b \'93
\f5\b0 I am not human. I am the biological manifestation of the company\'92s will. In the name of stability I destabilize. In the name of order I deliver chaos. In the name of life I bring death.\'94 
\f4\i0 He suddenly charged at her, quickly and malevolently, the nasty scowl upon his face a clear sign of his intent to do harm. She did not move, but stared him down, unflinching as a wry smile began to inch across of her face. As he approached with images of beating the life out of her dancing in his brain, he found he could not raise his arms and fists would not form. He tried for his gun, but it was just too heavy. He became most confused. Was this some sudden rush of morality flooding his body, washing clean the cankered crust of his licentious, black soul? He rejected that idea outright. He thought she must be just too good looking to hit, but that seemed unlikely too. He rather enjoyed messing up a beautiful face. They were just so damned punch-able and the looks of shock and surprise were just priceless. The truth, he seemed unable to recognise or refused to face, was she frightened him. He had, unconsciously, sensed the danger, the truth of this petite girl \'96 that he did not stand a chance against her.\
\'93What happened to all his stuff?\'94 He said, retreating towards to the door.\
\'93The office has been completely cleared out. Company property has been returned, his personal property has been sent home and the rest was thrown out.\'94\
\'93The cleaners?\'94\
\'93Well, a cleaner, yes.\'94\
He left without a word, remembering the cleaner on the elevator not 10 minutes prior. Sarah reported the strange man to Security, but she knew that would do no good. Re-runs of \'93Random Camera L1 - L3\'94 were showing on the link and box-headed security types loved that crap. She hacked into the security system and began downloading surveillance logs herself, but found she had to call on all her hacking magic to reconstitute the files, all damaged and jumbled into white noise. The security system had clearly been compromised and when she finally put Humpty together again, she could not find a trace of the man anywhere. The security footage of the corridor outside her office did not detect this man at all. At no time, it appeared, did this phantom enter her office, her vivid memory of their conversation notwithstanding. \
Normal people, average punters, cannot so easily circumvent high level security systems. He was clearly no ordinary man and the only people who could get their hands on that sort of technology were Intelligence Agents. 
\f5\i Was that a gun he was packing? Tax me into poverty! I sent this loon after the cleaner. 
\f4\i0 She knew she should not give a shit about the level 2 garbage collector, she\'92d only just met him, but she did care and did not know why. A quick check of the security logs revealing Cracker had not yet exited the business district, trebling her concern. Once he was in \'93The Pale\'94 he would be hard to follow, but he had to get there first.\
Cracker was still looking up at the layer, shaking his head and grumbling, \'93What a fuck-up.\'94 The layer was constant reminder of the line drawn between the lows and upper society. He really hated the layer and what stuck in his craw was the fact that the very people that engineered this blunder were the same people that live above and beyond its rancid reality. 
\f5\i Commit a crime against all humanity and receive thanks from a grateful nation. 
\f4\i0 \
Still looking up and cursing the layer, Cracker didn\'92t notice the dead male on the grubby pitted cement. He tripped over the body of some poor sod, accidentally landing right on top. At close range Cracker noticed the man was wearing an expensive suit and it looked new, but upon second glance his eyes were drawn to a pair of grimy, calloused hands adorned with oddly bent fingers and filthy nails. This was no L.5. Others on street level chose not to notice the dead body in their midst and merely strode an elliptical path around the gristly scene. The crowd un-gathered remarkably fast and while nobody was looking Cracker pocketed anything he could find. \
- - -\
Operational support guys, the underlings of the intelligence community, were always working on covert ops and difficult to locate. They were the guys that broke into houses to place covert listening devices, cameras and bombs - they undertook physical surveillance, they did all the jobs no one else wanted to do \'96 in this case they were to collect the body of a blank. They kept an eyeball on their target and watched as he dropped like a stone. Initially they thought him to be shot, but did not see the discharge flare of a particle weapon nor was anyone close enough to have brought him down by other means. He just seemed to lose consciousness and fell where he stood. \
\'91That\'92s our cue. Let\'92s pick him up\'94 the driver had said. \
\'93No. Where\'92s the square head? We can\'92t pick him up without the green light from him.\'94 The other shot back.\
\'93We are here to pick up a dead body. Does he look dead to you?\'94\
\'93He\'92s not moving. Wait a minute, there\'92s some good Samaritan trying to help.\'94\
\'93The idiot in the busted overalls? He\'92s going to fuck this up for us. Let\'92s roll.\'94\
The van was multi-purpose surveillance rig, currently disguised as a coronial pick-up unit. There were so many dead and dying on the street at any one time, it became necessary for these \'93meat-wagons\'94 to permanently scour the thoroughfares of the city, scooping up the lifeless before they became too rancid. There were so many, that an extra van floating about would be noticed by no one. The driver kicked the small motor into life and the van slid silently to where the blank lay. \
As Cracker casually rose he was confronted by a Coroner\'92s meat-wagon driver and his body-snatching off-sider. \
\'93I was just about to call you guys. I don\'92t think he\'92s been dead for long \'96 he\'92s still warm.\'94 Cracker said in the most sycophantic tone he could muster.\
 \
\'93Yeah, yeah. Fuck off, pal\'94 They said in unison.\
Cracker took his cue and backed away, joining the throng of mindless automatons and marched towards the security gates. In the Primary Trade Dominion, security guards weren\'92t known for their high intelligence. Most realised they were going nowhere in life and were happy to get a gig sitting around doing fuck-all most of the time and the rest of the time you got to mess with people\'92s heads. They were all recruited from level 1 ranks and were nothing in the scheme of things, but when you stepped into their little bailiwick, they were the law. Even though, Cracker was a regular in the business district, businessmen certainly weren\'92t taking out their own trash, security always made him jump through all the hoops. \
\'93Place your bag, or sack, is it? On the scanner. Work Visa?\'94 Like everyone else, Cracker did not like imitation cops looking down their noses at him. For that matter he didn\'92t like real cops acting superior either, but then again, they could shoot you just for the hell of it.\
\'93Yep.\'94\
\'93Retinal scan or implant?\'94 \
\'93Tattoo.\'94 Cracker thrust out his left arm, palm up.\
\'93This is old technology. You must up-grade to retinal scanning or get an implant. Can I arrange an appointment for you?\'94 The guard did his best impression of a desperate vacuum cleaner salesman.\
\'93We have this discussion every time. It is way too expensive and I know you are just trying to sell product. Do you get kickbacks? Do you get more for the retinal tweaking or implant surgery?\'94\
\'93Place your arm in the scanner, sir.\'94 The guard replied with impertinence, managing to alter his inflection and tone so as to alter the meaning of \'91sir\'92 to \'91turd on the ground.\'92\
\'93Its implants isn\'92t it?\'94 Cracker quizzed as he waved his arm over the scanner. \'93Going under the knife every time there is a new model \'96 the kick-backs must pile up, tax free, I bet.\'94 The guard was tight lipped and more than a little upset. He did not like his integrity questioned, especially when it was plain he had questionable integrity. \
\'93You can go.\'94 The guard pointed to the exit. \'93Now.\'94\
\'93Is it true that they can force a customer to undergo surgery against their will?\'94 Cracker was smiling widely. He knew the stop-go man was rattled.\
\'93It\'92s a service contract, damn it! They know what they are getting into!\'94 Retorted the red faced gate keeper.\
Cracker laughed and wandered slowly through the gargantuan exit gates and into The Pale, a dark ghetto bounded on all sides by office blocks and high class apartment buildings, hundreds of stories high, rising through the layer and making a reasonable bid for outer-space. The Pale was the home of the unwanted and unnecessary human refuse of the great Primary Trade Dominion. The crime rate was unconscionably high and consequently the police rarely made a visit. Law enforcement was engaged in shielding the rich from the poor, not protecting the poor from each other. The poor could eat shit and die. In the Primary Trade Dominion, egalitarians were those who only ate eagle. \
* * *\
Another check on the security records showed Cracker had finally made it out of the business district and into the Pale. Relieved, Sarah decided to do a little more digging and spent the rest of the morning interrogating the link and breaking into data-bases, gathering all known information about Tony Hardy. Unusually, however, she found that there was very little information stored about him at all. Most of the entries had been recently deleted, quite a few that very morning. No matter how hard she looked she could uncover almost no information pertaining to Tony (Cracker) Hardy. A birth certificate noting \'91Father Unknown\'92, some old school records, a business listing for Cracker Jack Cleaning and some fines for drunk and disorderly. That was about it. There were no expenditure analysis records, not one of the many thousands of advertising algorithms had ever sent him any spam and most unusually, there were no records of any malware attacks against him. \
Sarah sat still in silent contemplation, allowing her eyes to fade in and out of focus as she stared out the window, until she sported a blank thousand-yard stare. She mustered all of the processing power of her towering intellect, but drew no firm conclusions. On another day she might have shrugged off the creepy visit by the foul company agent, but he took off after cleaner with a hateful fervor. Something odd was afoot and this Cracker person seemed to be where the lines converged. She had, to this point, managed to elude the guilt of sending an armed intelligence agent after an unarmed cleaner, but it now began to advance in giant ripples of shame and dread. Outwardly, of course, she was ice cool. The legacy of a proper education and damned fine breeding, so she would have you believe. The truth, she never discussed for a number of reasons, was her mother managed to jag a rich boyfriend who had her safely squared away in boarding school the moment he moved in.\
When 4.00pm approached she packed up for the day and took a hover-shuttle to the Elite-Management Medical Facility where she gave samples, underwent embarrassing and probing examinations and bombarded with radiation of all types from an army of scanners. She was in perfect health and was approved for promotion to Level 5. It amused her the results noted she was not pregnant. She had no intention of becoming a mother any time soon.
